UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 2: Role play and freeze frames', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story. Choose a moment, decide who each person is, and show it with body shape, facial expression and the distance between people. Hold still for five seconds and breathe quietly. A thought bubble is when the leader taps a person and they say what their character is thinking. Be careful, and never touch or push others.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a freeze frame of ''the winning goal'' for a group of three. Say who each person is and what pose and face each would show.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked face.'' Different roles at different levels make the image clear, and each pose shows an emotion.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate three freeze frames for a story, such as the beginning, middle and end of a picnic. Draw or photograph each, write a thought bubble for one character in each and perform them for family. Stay safe and do not touch others.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story. Choose a moment, decide who each person is, and show it with body shape, facial expression and the distance between people. Hold still for five seconds and breathe quietly. A thought bubble is when the leader taps a person and they say what their character is thinking. Be careful, and never touch or push others.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create three freeze frames for a story, such as the beginning, middle and end of a picnic. Draw or photograph each, write a thought bubble for one character in each and perform them for family. Stay safe and do not touch Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a freeze frame on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.. Correction: Strong answer: ''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a freeze frame on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a freeze frame, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a freeze frame with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a freeze frame. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a freeze frame. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"Plan a freeze frame of ''the winning goal'' for a group of three. Say who each person is and what pose and face each would show. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a freeze frame; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked","Careful use of a still image and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a freeze frame?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a still image and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply a freeze frame to new material and name the a still image and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","Name a still image and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a freeze frame."],"question":"Which response best applies a freeze frame with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a freeze frame. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked"],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for a freeze frame?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a freeze frame.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a freeze frame?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a freeze frame?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a still image and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Create three freeze frames for a story, such as the beginning, middle and end of a picnic.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling."],"question":"Which independent response best applies a freeze frame to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a freeze frame with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a freeze frame on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a freeze frame on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a freeze frame. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a freeze frame, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling.. Correction: Strong answer: ''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w11-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a freeze frame.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use a freeze frame with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a still image and explain why it matters.","I can practise a role play independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a thought bubble needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story.","options":["A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a freeze frame with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Another points. A third stands with hands on cheeks looking relieved. When the teacher taps the first child, she says, ''I thought I''d never see you again!'' The pose and the thought show the moment clearly.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a freeze frame of ''the winning goal'' for a group of three. Say who each person is and what pose and face each would show.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked face.'' Different roles at different levels make the image clear, and each pose shows an emotion.","explain":"A freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story. Choose a moment, decide who each person is, and show it with body shape, facial expression and the distance between people. Hold still for five seconds and breathe quietly. A thought bubble is when the leader taps a person and they say what their character is thinking. Be careful, and never touch or push others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A freeze frame and thought bubble.","caption":"Hold still and speak.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Still image</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hold like a photograph</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Thought</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tap and say a thought</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a freeze frame with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Another points. A third stands with hands on cheeks looking relieved. When the teacher taps the first child, she says, ''I thought I''d never see you again!'' The pose and the thought show the moment clearly. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Levels and faces in a freeze frame.","caption":"Use levels.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Levels</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">kneel, stand, dive</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Faces</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">proud, shocked, joyful</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a freeze frame with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a group makes a freeze frame of ''the lost kitten found''. One child kneels with arms out, wide eyes and a smile. Another points. A third stands with hands on cheeks looking relieved. When the teacher taps the first child, she says, ''I thought I''d never see you again!'' The pose and the thought show the moment clearly.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''One player in a kicking pose with a proud face, one with both arms up cheering, one goalkeeper diving with a shocked face.'' Different roles at different levels make the image clear, and each pose shows an emotion.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that a freeze frame is just standing still and smiling. A good still image shows the moment and feelings through body shape and expression, and every person has a role. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a freeze frame with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA freeze frame, also called a still image, is a pose where actors hold still like a photograph to show a moment in a story. Choose a moment, decide who each person is, and show it with body shape, facial expression and the distance between people. Hold still for five seconds and breathe quietly. A thought bubble is when the leader taps a person and they say what their character is thinking. Be careful, and never touch or push others. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","